\section{Data Type Vocabulary}{

Where a page says which data types an argument takes, it names a group of
them with a single word:
\itemize{
\item \emph{any data type} -- all of them: \code{bool}, the signed and unsigned integers,
and the floats.
\item \emph{numeric} -- signed integer, unsigned integer and float.
\item \emph{integer} -- signed and unsigned integer.
\item \emph{integerish} -- boolean and integer, signed or unsigned.
\item \emph{signed numeric} -- signed integer and float.
\item \emph{float} -- the whole float category: \code{f32}, \code{f64} and any narrower float
a backend supports.
\item \emph{boolean} -- \code{bool}, the only member of its category.
}
}

\section{Where a Data Type Comes From}{

An R value has no data type of its own. Where nothing in the program says
which one it should take, it commits to the default of its category, which
\code{\link[=default_dtypes]{default_dtypes()}} reports and the \code{anvl.default_dtypes} option
configures. \code{\link[=peek_dtype]{peek_dtype()}} reports the default a given R value would
commit to.

The same defaults settle the data type of a result anvl chooses on its own,
where no R value is involved at all: an index (\code{nv_argmax()},
\code{nv_argsort()}, \code{nv_top_k()}, the cumulative extrema, \code{nv_lu()}'s pivots),
the accumulator a boolean input is counted at (\code{nv_reduce_sum()},
\code{nv_reduce_prod()}, \code{nv_cumsum()}, \code{nv_cumprod()}, \code{nv_trace()}), and the
float a non-float input is averaged or interpolated at (\code{nv_mean()},
\code{nv_var()}, \code{nv_sd()}, \code{nv_median()}, \code{nv_quantile()}).

Within its own category an R value assumes the data type it meets instead,
and is built at it directly rather than converted to it, which is what keeps
\code{nv_scalar(1, "f64") / sqrt(2)} exact. The primitives require operands that
have a data type to agree on it; the \verb{nv_*} functions promote them to a
common one.
